  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mfenced><msub><mi>CH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mfenced><mn>3</mn></msub><mover><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mo>+</mo></mover><mo>-</mo><mtext>Tertiary carbocation</mtext><mspace linebreak=\"newline\"></mspace><msub><mfenced><msub><mi>CH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msub><mover><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mo>+</mo></mover><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mo>-</mo><mtext>Secondary carbocation</mtext><mspace linebreak=\"newline\"></mspace><msub><mi>CH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>-</mo><mover><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mo>+</mo></mover><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>-</mo><mtext>Primary carbocation</mtext><mspace linebreak=\"newline\"></mspace><mover><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mo>+</mo></mover><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>-</mo><mtext>Methyl carbocation</mtext></math></p><p>Stability of carbocations depends upon the electron releasing groups. The given carbocations stability can be explained by hyperconjugation effect. More the number of alpha hydrogen, more are number of hyperconjugation structures, hence, more is the stability of carbocation.</p><p>Thus, the observed order of stability for carbocations is as follows: tertiary &gt; secondary &gt; primary &gt; methyl</p><p>Hence, option C is correct.</p></blockquote>"
